<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  FrameForge VM test harness :: autounattend template - Windows 11, x64, ja-JP.

  TOKENS. New-TestVm.ps1 replaces these before writing the file into the unattend ISO:
      {{COMPUTERNAME}}  guest computer name (also the domain half of the PowerShell Direct credential)
      {{USERNAME}}      local administrator account the harness logs in as
      {{PASSWORD}}      that account's password
      {{IMAGE_NAME}}    the /IMAGE/NAME value, e.g. "Windows 11 Pro" - selects the edition out of a
                        multi-edition install.wim WITHOUT depending on an index that shifts between
                        ISO revisions
  Any token left unreplaced makes the file invalid on purpose: New-TestVm.ps1 scans the substituted
  text for a leftover double-brace sequence and refuses to build the ISO if it finds one. That is
  also why this comment does not spell one out literally.

  WHY A LOCAL ACCOUNT IN THE oobeSystem PASS IS THE WHOLE POINT
  ========================================
  Windows 11 OOBE demands a Microsoft account on consumer editions. The workaround everyone
  knows - `oobe\bypassnro.cmd` at the network screen - WAS REMOVED in recent 24H2/25H2 builds,
  so a harness built on it silently stops working on exactly the newest cells it most needs to
  cover. Defining <LocalAccounts> here plus HideOnlineAccountScreens is not a bypass at all: it
  is the supported unattended path, OOBE never reaches the account screen, and it works
  identically on 23H2, 24H2 and 25H2. That is why every cell in this matrix provisions this way.

  PASSWORD IN CLEAR TEXT - READ THIS
  ========================================
  <PlainText>true</PlainText> is deliberate. The obfuscated form is a base64 of the UTF-16LE
  password concatenated with an element-dependent suffix, and getting that suffix wrong produces
  a VM that cannot be logged into for reasons nothing reports. Clear text is honest about what
  it is. Consequences, which you accept by using this harness:
    * The unattend ISO on D:\ffvm\unattend contains the password. New-TestVm.ps1 deletes it after
      the base install unless -KeepUnattendIso is passed.
    * Windows scrubs passwords out of C:\Windows\Panther\unattend.xml at the end of OOBE, but do
      not rely on that.
    * These VMs must never be given a routable network path to anything you care about, and the
      password must not be one you use anywhere else.

  POWERSHELL DIRECT needs nothing configured in the guest beyond a working local administrator
  account and the vmicvmsession integration service (on by default). The FirstLogonCommands below
  set the execution policy so the copied-in .ps1 files run, and drop a provisioning marker the
  host reads to confirm the install genuinely finished rather than inferring it from the first
  moment PowerShell Direct answers.

  DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: `powercfg /h off`. Hibernation and Fast Startup availability are signals
  health.ps1 measures (the powercfg /a parse at ~830 is one of the confirmed English-text sites),
  so the harness must not change them during provisioning.

  THIS IS THE ja-JP CELL. Everything the de-DE cell changes, plus the encoding layer:
    * InputLocale 0411:00000411 is the Japanese IME. A ja-JP OOBE otherwise stops on an input
      method page that no amount of HideXxx elements skips, and the cell hangs at first boot.
    * SystemLocale ja-JP sets the system OEM code page to 932 (Shift-JIS). This is the value that
      decides how PowerShell 5.1 decodes the stdout of native tools such as sfc.exe, fsutil.exe,
      w32tm.exe and DISM. When it is wrong the text arrives as mojibake, so even a regex that was
      correctly translated fails to match - which is why "add the German strings" is not a fix.
    * TimeZone Tokyo Standard Time (UTC+9, no DST).
  Use a LOCALIZED Japanese ISO, not a language pack over en-US.
  VERIFY {{IMAGE_NAME}} against `dism /Get-WimInfo` before the first base build.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

  <settings pass="windowsPE">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>ja-JP</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>0411:00000411</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>ja-JP</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>ja-JP</UILanguage>
      <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
      <UserLocale>ja-JP</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <!-- WillShowUI never: an unattended install that stops on a disk prompt is a hung cell. -->
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <!-- Gen 2 / UEFI / GPT layout. No separate recovery partition: WinRE lands in the
                 Windows partition, which is fine for a test cell and saves a partition. -->
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <Type>EFI</Type>
              <Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <Type>MSR</Type>
              <Size>128</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <Type>Primary</Type>
              <Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Label>System</Label>
              <Format>FAT32</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter>
              <Format>NTFS</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/NAME</Key>
              <Value>{{IMAGE_NAME}}</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <!-- An EMPTY <Key/> is what suppresses the product-key page while leaving the machine
             unlicensed. That is intentional: the activation cell needs a genuinely unactivated
             Windows so activation-retry has a real fault to detect. -->
        <ProductKey>
          <Key></Key>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <FullName>{{USERNAME}}</FullName>
        <Organization>FrameForge VM Test</Organization>
      </UserData>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">
    <!-- LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy = 1, set HERE and not by guest\setup\Initialize-Guest.ps1.

         WHY THE UNATTEND AND NOT THE GUEST SCRIPT
         PowerShell Direct authenticates a LOCAL account. This unattend creates a local account
         and adds it to Administrators - it is NOT the built-in RID-500 Administrator, and a
         non-RID-500 local admin reaching a machine non-interactively is subject to UAC remote
         restrictions: the session holds a filtered token, so every elevated probe in health.ps1
         degrades to 'needs-admin' and every mutating repair refuses. Invoke-VmMatrix.ps1 proves
         the session is elevated before it concludes anything, and skips the cell if it is not.

         Initialize-Guest.ps1 used to set this value itself, which was circular and could never
         have worked on a first run: writing it needs the elevated token it exists to grant, and
         so do the two steps before it (Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope LocalMachine and
         Enable-ComputerRestore, both HKLM writes). A script cannot bootstrap the privilege it
         needs in order to bootstrap that privilege.

         RunSynchronousCommand in the specialize pass runs as LOCAL SYSTEM, before any user has
         logged on and long before PowerShell Direct is used, so it has the rights unconditionally
         and the circularity does not exist. Initialize-Guest.ps1 now VERIFIES the value instead of
         setting it, and refuses if it is absent - so a base image built from an older template
         fails loudly rather than producing cells whose every row looks like an engine failure.

         THIS IS A REAL SECURITY CHANGE, and it is the only one this harness makes. It applies to
         throwaway VMs that are built with no network adapter attached (see New-TestVm.ps1). Do not
         reuse this template for a machine that matters. -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>PowerShell Direct needs an unfiltered token for the local admin account (see the comment above)</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System" /v LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <ComputerName>{{COMPUTERNAME}}</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>Tokyo Standard Time</TimeZone>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <InputLocale>0411:00000411</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>ja-JP</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>ja-JP</UILanguage>
      <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
      <UserLocale>ja-JP</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
        <SkipMachineOOBE>false</SkipMachineOOBE>
        <SkipUserOOBE>false</SkipUserOOBE>
      </OOBE>

      <UserAccounts>
        <LocalAccounts>
          <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
            <Name>{{USERNAME}}</Name>
            <DisplayName>{{USERNAME}}</DisplayName>
            <Group>Administrators</Group>
            <Password>
              <Value>{{PASSWORD}}</Value>
              <PlainText>true</PlainText>
            </Password>
          </LocalAccount>
        </LocalAccounts>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!-- One automatic logon so the first-logon commands run without a console operator.
           LogonCount 1: after that the VM sits at the lock screen, which is all PowerShell
           Direct needs (it does not require an interactive session). -->
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <LogonCount>1</LogonCount>
        <Username>{{USERNAME}}</Username>
        <Password>
          <Value>{{PASSWORD}}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
      </AutoLogon>

      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Allow the harness's copied-in scripts to run</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope LocalMachine -Force"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>Never sleep or blank the display mid-run (hibernation is left ALONE on purpose)</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c powercfg /change standby-timeout-ac 0 &amp; powercfg /change monitor-timeout-ac 0</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Description>Create the harness working directories</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd.exe /c md C:\ffvmtest\ff &amp; md C:\ffvmtest\guest\faults &amp; md C:\ffvmtest\state &amp; md C:\ffvmtest\out</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Description>Provisioning marker the host reads to confirm the install actually finished</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$b=(Get-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion'); ('{0}.{1} {2} {3}' -f $b.CurrentBuildNumber,$b.UBR,$b.DisplayVersion,$b.EditionID) | Set-Content -Encoding ASCII C:\ffvmtest\provisioned.txt"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
  </settings>

</unattend>
